<title>BELGIUM: Lower wheat price may lead to export tax cut - trade.</title>
<headline>Lower wheat price may lead to export tax cut - trade.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-05-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Falling wheat prices may soon encourage the European Union to cut wheat export taxes, EU grain trade sources said.</p>
<p>The EU on May 14 introduced taxes of 15 ecus a tonne on daily exports of soft wheat grain and 20 ecus a tonne on wheat flour in a bid to stop prices overheating.</p>
<p>&quot;We hope that it will be reduced because it does not reflect actual markets as prices are down both in the United States and Europe,&quot; said one source.</p>
<p>The source said that EU policy of taxing wheat exports and imposing duties on imports was contradictory.</p>
<p>The tax was introduced as a precautionary move following rising prices in recent weeks due to low stocks and weather worries about harvests. It also ended 1996/97 wheat exports.</p>
<p>But markets have since swung round as weather fears eased and on new forecasts of large crops. --Brussels newsroom +322 287 6830</p>
</text>
